WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:13.640
You know, usually when you picture the architecture of the digital world, like the software running your bank, your car, your phone, there is this comforting expectation of precision.

00:14.200 --> 00:16.260
Right. Yeah. You want it to be exact.

00:16.600 --> 00:20.340
Exactly. It feels like it should operate like structural engineering.

00:20.340 --> 00:31.580
I mean, if you look at a blueprint of a skyscraper, you can trace exactly how the weight of the roof transfers, you know, down the steel columns into the foundation and finally into the bedrock.

00:31.880 --> 00:33.520
It's visible. Yeah. It's documented.

00:33.840 --> 00:35.500
Yeah. And above all, it's a closed system.

00:36.040 --> 00:41.700
Right. It feels totally deterministic. I mean, the steel beam is either rated for the load or it isn't.

00:42.020 --> 00:45.040
And the engineer knows exactly where every single beam came from.

00:45.040 --> 00:54.740
But then you step into the actual reality of modern software development, specifically, and this is what we're getting into today, the world of software dependencies.

00:55.340 --> 00:58.960
And suddenly that pristine blueprint is completely illegible.

00:59.500 --> 00:59.940
Oh, it's a mess.

01:00.240 --> 01:03.760
It really is. You aren't just building with your own steel beams anymore.

01:03.760 --> 01:15.220
You are automatically importing thousands of beams overnight from, like, total strangers all over the world, trusting that they are solid metal and not, say, hollow plastic filled with termites.

01:15.360 --> 01:16.480
Which is a terrifying thought.

01:16.780 --> 01:20.020
It is. We are looking at a landscape that is incredibly murky.

01:20.280 --> 01:26.320
And if you are joining us today, it's because you want to understand these complex, unseen systems running our digital lives.

01:26.420 --> 01:30.620
And you want to do it without getting buried in impenetrable computer science jargon.

01:30.800 --> 01:32.880
Yeah, we're going to keep it grounded.

01:32.880 --> 01:33.360
Exactly.

01:33.800 --> 01:38.540
So today we are taking a deep dive into a piece of independent research by Flyxion.

01:39.060 --> 01:43.260
It's titled, Percolation Screening on the Cross-Boundary Shadow Graph.

01:43.420 --> 01:43.940
Quite a title.

01:44.240 --> 01:45.120
I know, right?

01:45.640 --> 01:47.760
But our mission for this deep dive is actually pretty clear.

01:48.080 --> 01:56.320
We are going to explore how advanced network theory and complex mathematics are being used to map the hidden vulnerabilities in software dependencies.

01:56.320 --> 02:06.040
And we are tracking how a single malicious command, just buried deep in a library you didn't even know you downloaded, might find a secret tunnel straight to your most sensitive data.

02:06.040 --> 02:17.600
Yeah, and the central tension here is how this paper takes these highly abstract mathematical concepts, like percolation theory, which traditionally I think studies how liquids flow through porous materials, right?

02:17.780 --> 02:18.460
Right, exactly.

02:18.940 --> 02:20.460
And the evolution of random graphs.

02:20.460 --> 02:24.740
It takes those and applies them to a very urgent practical question.

02:24.920 --> 02:25.440
Which is?

02:25.940 --> 02:28.220
How structurally vulnerable is our code really?

02:28.220 --> 02:38.040
It bridges pure mathematics and real-world cybersecurity, but it does so by forcing us to question how we even define a map of our software in the first place.

02:38.620 --> 02:40.580
Okay, let's start right there with the map.

02:40.960 --> 02:45.540
Because before we can even begin to find a vulnerability, we need to map the territory.

02:45.780 --> 02:49.980
But the source material immediately points out that a single map just isn't enough.

02:50.120 --> 02:50.840
No, it's really not.

02:51.180 --> 02:55.320
To understand what's happening, we actually need to look at two completely different interpretations of the system.

02:55.320 --> 03:02.420
Yeah, the foundation of this entire analysis rests on distinguishing between two distinct models of the software ecosystem.

03:03.040 --> 03:08.260
The baseline model is what the author calls the full graph, which is denoted mathematically as G.

03:08.600 --> 03:08.880
Okay.

03:09.180 --> 03:11.500
So think of a graph in the mathematical sense.

03:11.960 --> 03:16.140
You have vertices, which are dots, and edges, which are the lines connecting them.

03:16.800 --> 03:20.040
In the full graph, the vertices are the software symbols.

03:20.520 --> 03:21.540
By symbols, you mean?

03:21.880 --> 03:25.260
The individual functions, the classes, the specific variables.

03:25.920 --> 03:28.180
The actual microscopic building blocks of the code.

03:28.500 --> 03:35.080
So if I write a function called calculateTax, that function is just one dot on the graph.

03:35.220 --> 03:35.660
Precisely.

03:36.020 --> 03:40.500
And the edges, the lines connecting the dots, represent the functional relationships between them.

03:40.660 --> 03:40.900
Right.

03:41.100 --> 03:48.400
So if your calculateTax function calls another function named getTaxRate, an edge is drawn from the first to the second.

03:48.940 --> 03:52.640
This applies to memory references, shared types, inheritance, all of it.

03:52.640 --> 03:53.360
Got it.

03:53.360 --> 04:01.020
So the full graph is basically the exhaustive, microscopic picture of every single relationship in the software.

04:01.360 --> 04:01.560
Yes.

04:02.020 --> 04:08.020
It maps the internal logic inside a single package of code, and it maps the external calls between different packages.

04:08.020 --> 04:12.340
Okay, let's unpack this, because that brings us to the second map.

04:12.680 --> 04:20.300
If the full graph is the complete picture, we then have what the paper calls the shadow graph, or G shadow.

04:20.660 --> 04:20.920
Right.

04:20.920 --> 04:22.340
Let's use an analogy here.

04:22.740 --> 04:25.520
Imagine the full graph is a map of an entire country.

04:25.520 --> 04:33.560
It shows every single interstate highway, but it also maps every local street, every tiny alleyway, every dirt road, and every private driveway.

04:34.020 --> 04:35.740
It is overwhelmingly detailed.

04:35.880 --> 04:36.440
Yeah, exactly.

04:37.060 --> 04:41.480
The shadow graph takes that massive map and applies a huge filter to it.

04:41.820 --> 04:44.060
It completely erases all the local roads.

04:44.060 --> 04:46.840
It only shows you the highways that cross state lines.

04:47.280 --> 04:48.500
That's a really good way to visualize it.

04:49.120 --> 04:53.840
In the language of the paper, the shadow graph only retains edges that cross a package boundary.

04:54.520 --> 04:56.140
So internal stuff is just gone.

04:56.460 --> 04:56.780
Exactly.

04:57.400 --> 05:05.640
If a function calls another function within the exact same software package, say two internal helper functions talking to each other, that line is just deleted.

05:05.840 --> 05:06.020
Okay.

05:06.020 --> 05:09.720
You only see the connections jumping from one distinct package to another.

05:10.460 --> 05:17.060
You strip away the internal logic of the individual components to look purely at how the different components interact at a macro level.

05:17.740 --> 05:23.940
Which sounds great for simplifying things, but the text actually highlights a massive pitfall here.

05:24.120 --> 05:32.760
If you are a developer or a security researcher trying to secure a system, you cannot use the shadow graph to actually search for propagation paths.

05:33.000 --> 05:33.920
No, you really can't.

05:33.920 --> 05:39.800
You cannot use it to trace how a real attack might move, which honestly feels totally backwards to me.

05:39.960 --> 05:46.220
If it shows the connections between packages, wouldn't that be exactly what you want to track a supply chain attack?

05:46.340 --> 05:48.160
I mean, the attack comes from outside, right?

05:48.820 --> 05:55.600
The attack originates outside, yes, but its route is highly dependent on the internal roads you just erased.

05:55.920 --> 05:56.320
Oh.

05:56.320 --> 06:04.100
Yeah, the source provides this really devastating example of why searching for paths on the shadow graph fundamentally fails.

06:05.420 --> 06:10.240
Imagine a malicious dependency calls a public function located inside package A.

06:10.400 --> 06:10.680
Okay.

06:11.160 --> 06:11.480
Tracking.

06:11.860 --> 06:15.660
Now, that public function in package A doesn't do the sensitive work itself.

06:16.140 --> 06:21.560
It turns around and calls an internal helper function, which is also located inside package A.

06:21.880 --> 06:22.980
Right, on a local road.

06:22.980 --> 06:23.420
Exactly.

06:24.320 --> 06:34.420
Finally, that internal helper function reaches out and invokes a highly sensitive operation, like, say, deleting a file in a completely different package, package B.

06:34.680 --> 06:34.940
Okay.

06:35.320 --> 06:35.580
Okay.

06:35.860 --> 06:43.520
So the path goes from the malicious source, it goes into public A, and then it goes through internal A and out to sensitive B.

06:43.760 --> 06:44.040
Right.

06:44.220 --> 06:44.480
Okay.

06:45.200 --> 06:47.540
Now, look at that through the lens of the full graph.

06:47.640 --> 06:49.540
You see that continuous path perfectly.

06:49.900 --> 06:50.840
Every step is drawn.

06:50.840 --> 06:52.580
Right, because all the local roads are there.

06:52.820 --> 06:53.080
Yes.

06:53.200 --> 06:53.380
Okay.

06:53.680 --> 06:53.920
Yeah.

06:54.300 --> 06:56.660
But remember the rule of the shadow graph.

06:56.880 --> 06:58.900
It deletes all internal edges.

06:59.460 --> 07:05.960
So that vital link between the public function in package A and the internal helper in package A is completely erased from the map.

07:06.220 --> 07:06.660
Ugh.

07:08.120 --> 07:08.600
Oh.

07:09.540 --> 07:10.440
Oh, wow.

07:10.440 --> 07:15.180
So in the shadow graph, you'll see a line coming into package A from the attacker, right?

07:15.320 --> 07:21.440
And a totally separate line leaving package A, going to the sensitive file deleter, but they literally don't connect.

07:21.780 --> 07:22.220
Exactly.

07:22.220 --> 07:26.440
The appear as disconnected endpoints.

07:27.040 --> 07:32.620
The path is broken on the map, even though it functions perfectly in the real compiled software.

07:32.840 --> 07:33.680
That's wild.

07:34.040 --> 07:35.920
The author is very firm about this, too.

07:36.260 --> 07:39.820
Failing to find a threat in the shadow graph is not a sound negative test.

07:40.280 --> 07:44.440
Just because the shadow graph shows no complete root doesn't mean you are safe.

07:44.440 --> 07:56.140
Well, I have to push back here, then, because if filtering out the internal edges actively breaks the attack paths and hides the vulnerabilities, why on earth would we even bother building the shadow graph?

07:56.380 --> 07:57.180
That's a fair question.

07:57.280 --> 07:59.460
It sounds like we're just deliberately breaking our own map.

07:59.560 --> 08:01.320
Why not just use the full graph all the time?

08:01.680 --> 08:08.280
Because the full graph is computationally overwhelming and often way too noisy to understand the systemic risk.

08:08.820 --> 08:12.240
The shadow graph isn't meant for finding a specific attacker's path.

08:12.240 --> 08:14.360
Its job is purely macroscopic.

08:15.100 --> 08:15.780
Macroscopic.

08:16.260 --> 08:17.320
Like, big picture.

08:17.540 --> 08:17.740
Right.

08:18.140 --> 08:21.800
It is designed to measure the structural exposure of the entire system.

08:22.300 --> 08:23.880
It answers a completely different question.

08:24.280 --> 08:26.900
How much boundary crossing is happening overall?

08:26.980 --> 08:27.500
Oh, I see.

08:27.740 --> 08:32.380
Is your software architecture highly modular, where packages mind their own business?

08:32.920 --> 08:38.240
Or is it a tangled mess, where every package is constantly whispering to every other package?

08:38.260 --> 08:38.620
Gotcha.

08:38.800 --> 08:42.540
The shadow graph gives you the big picture of your system's porosity.

08:42.540 --> 08:56.140
But the paper concludes that when you shift from assessing general risk to doing actual weakness searches, meaning looking for specific actionable attack routes, you have to revert to the full graph, where every internal road is intact.

08:56.140 --> 08:57.520
Okay, that makes sense.

08:57.520 --> 09:07.220
It's like the difference between looking at climate data to see if you live in a hurricane zone versus looking at your house's specific blueprints to see if the roof will cave in.

09:07.340 --> 09:07.660
Exactly.

09:07.660 --> 09:12.180
You need the macro view for the threat landscape and the micro view to actually patch the hole.

09:12.720 --> 09:16.780
So, assuming we have these maps, we need to know how interconnected they really are.

09:17.220 --> 09:21.380
Are we looking at a software ecosystem that is basically a set of isolated islands?

09:21.380 --> 09:29.280
Or are we looking at one massive, globally connected supercontinent where a vulnerability in one tiny package could compromise everything?

09:29.280 --> 09:37.340
And to figure that out, we have to look at how things spread, which is where the paper introduces the Erdos-Renny evolution of random graphs.

09:37.820 --> 09:38.080
Right.

09:38.080 --> 09:47.280
To give some context, Paul Erdos and Alfred Drenny were mathematicians who, in the mid-20th century, basically invented the study of random networks.

09:48.020 --> 09:49.360
They asked a really simple question.

09:49.920 --> 09:57.700
If you have a bunch of isolated points and you randomly start drawing lines between them, at what point does the whole thing suddenly connect together?

09:58.020 --> 10:02.100
Like dropping a bunch of buttons on the floor and randomly tying threads between pairs of them.

10:02.300 --> 10:06.100
Eventually, you'll pick up one button and the whole pile will just come up with it.

10:06.100 --> 10:07.360
That's a perfect analogy.

10:07.360 --> 10:12.020
And in classical Erdos-Renny theory, that transition happens very abruptly.

10:13.060 --> 10:17.360
But the paper quickly notes that classical models don't work for software.

10:18.160 --> 10:18.420
Why not?

10:18.800 --> 10:23.440
Because Erdos-Renny assumes every button has roughly the same chance of getting a thread.

10:24.020 --> 10:27.000
The resulting network has a bell curve distribution of connections.

10:27.220 --> 10:29.480
Most nodes have an average number of links.

10:30.200 --> 10:33.000
But software doesn't look like a bell curve.

10:33.340 --> 10:36.100
It has a heavy-tailed or power law distribution.

10:36.100 --> 10:37.100
Right, right.

10:37.160 --> 10:41.360
Because you have thousands of tiny packages that maybe have one or two connections.

10:41.640 --> 10:50.380
And then you have massive utility libraries like requests or URL lib in Python that are imported by literally millions of other projects.

10:50.500 --> 10:51.980
They are massive hubs.

10:51.980 --> 10:52.700
Exactly.

10:52.700 --> 11:01.960
Because of those hubs, the author relies on something called the Molloy-Reed criterion, which is specifically tailored for networks with highly variable connection counts.

11:02.500 --> 11:02.620
Okay.

11:02.620 --> 11:08.440
The core of this criterion centers on a specific mathematical ratio denoted as K.

11:08.440 --> 11:11.540
Yeah, I was looking at this formula in the notes.

11:11.760 --> 11:16.860
K equals the average of the squared degrees divided by the average degree.

11:17.440 --> 11:22.860
And for anyone not steeped in graph theory, the degree of a dot is just how many lines connect to it.

11:23.080 --> 11:23.140
Right.

11:23.140 --> 11:26.000
But why square the degrees?

11:26.440 --> 11:30.820
If you want to know how connected a network is, why not just take the average number of connections?

11:31.360 --> 11:33.220
This is a crucial mechanical point.

11:33.780 --> 11:38.880
The text explicitly warrants that just taking the mean degree is a terrible summary for software.

11:39.520 --> 11:43.000
Let's actually do the math out loud to see why squaring them matters so much.

11:43.280 --> 11:43.520
Okay.

11:43.900 --> 11:44.360
Let's do it.

11:44.360 --> 11:46.900
Imagine a network with 10 packages.

11:47.680 --> 11:50.380
Nine of those packages only have one connection each.

11:50.940 --> 11:54.540
But the 10th package is a massive hub with 100 connections.

11:54.560 --> 11:54.700
Okay.

11:54.900 --> 11:57.980
So a total of 109 connections across 10 packages.

11:58.320 --> 12:00.440
The average degree is roughly 11.

12:00.780 --> 12:01.100
Right.

12:01.840 --> 12:05.160
And an average of 11 makes the network sound moderately connected.

12:05.680 --> 12:10.480
It completely masks the reality that there is one massive super hub controlling everything.

12:11.040 --> 12:12.580
Ah, I see where this is going.

12:12.580 --> 12:17.380
Now let's look at the numerator of the K-ratio, the average of the squared degrees.

12:18.180 --> 12:22.760
You square the ones, which stays one, but you square that 100 and it becomes 10,000.

12:22.860 --> 12:23.320
Oh, wow.

12:23.700 --> 12:23.920
Yeah.

12:24.560 --> 12:29.280
Suddenly, the math heavily penalizes or rather heavily highlights the hubs.

12:30.420 --> 12:35.380
By dividing the average of the squares by the standard average, the K-ratio mathematically

12:35.380 --> 12:37.780
forces those giant hubs to reveal themselves.

12:38.360 --> 12:42.100
It tells you how much the network relies on centralized choke points.

12:42.100 --> 12:43.900
That makes so much sense.

12:44.020 --> 12:48.060
It mathematically exposes the gravity of the big packages.

12:48.600 --> 12:53.820
And the paper states that if this K-ratio is greater than 2, the network enters the supercritical

12:53.820 --> 12:54.340
regime.

12:54.740 --> 12:57.980
What exactly does supercritical mean in this context?

12:58.400 --> 13:00.940
It means a giant component emerges.

13:01.420 --> 13:01.720
Okay.

13:01.900 --> 13:03.800
This isn't just a large cluster.

13:03.960 --> 13:08.240
It's a massive interconnected web that scales with the size of the whole network.

13:08.520 --> 13:09.260
So it's everywhere.

13:09.260 --> 13:14.720
Basically, if a software graph is supercritical, it means a huge portion of the code is mutually

13:14.720 --> 13:15.300
accessible.

13:15.920 --> 13:18.540
A virus or a vulnerability isn't isolated.

13:18.960 --> 13:21.460
It has the highways to travel almost anywhere.

13:21.860 --> 13:25.020
But the author doesn't just blindly trust this K-formula either.

13:25.280 --> 13:30.680
They offer a brilliant, specific example of how this mathematical threshold can trick you

13:30.680 --> 13:31.320
if you aren't careful.

13:31.320 --> 13:33.240
Yes, the archipelago example.

13:33.620 --> 13:33.940
Exactly.

13:34.400 --> 13:39.340
Let's walk through it because it shows the danger of relying on averages, even weighted

13:39.340 --> 13:40.000
averages.

13:40.680 --> 13:45.180
Imagine a graph made entirely of isolated four vertex cliques.

13:45.660 --> 13:45.920
Okay.

13:46.180 --> 13:47.580
So picture an archipelago.

13:47.860 --> 13:48.940
Little islands of code.

13:49.700 --> 13:52.540
On each island, there are exactly four dots.

13:53.220 --> 13:56.980
And on that specific island, every dot connects to the other three.

13:56.980 --> 13:57.020
Right.

13:57.480 --> 13:59.820
It's a perfectly interconnected little square.

14:00.200 --> 14:00.420
Right.

14:00.760 --> 14:05.580
But, and this is the key, none of the islands connect to each other.

14:06.060 --> 14:06.300
Right.

14:06.740 --> 14:10.100
So on every single island, every dot has exactly three connections.

14:10.500 --> 14:13.260
This means the average degree of the whole network is three.

14:13.820 --> 14:14.920
The square of three is nine.

14:15.440 --> 14:17.000
So the average of the squares is nine.

14:17.160 --> 14:17.680
Following you?

14:17.680 --> 14:23.660
If you run the Molloy-Reed math, nine divided by three, your k ratio is exactly three.

14:24.380 --> 14:25.820
And remember the rule we just talked about.

14:26.200 --> 14:31.720
If k is greater than two, the math says you are in the supercritical regime, and you should

14:31.720 --> 14:34.560
expect a massive, giant component connecting everything.

14:34.980 --> 14:36.260
But here, k is three.

14:36.820 --> 14:40.860
But if we look at our physical map of the archipelago, we just have a bunch of tiny,

14:41.020 --> 14:41.880
isolated islands.

14:42.220 --> 14:45.340
No single connected component is larger than four vertices.

14:45.940 --> 14:46.360
Exactly.

14:46.360 --> 14:51.660
The math says expect a giant continent, but reality is a bunch of rowboats.

14:52.360 --> 14:53.580
So why did the math fail?

14:54.100 --> 14:58.260
Well, it didn't fail so much as it meant an edge case it wasn't designed for.

14:58.720 --> 15:03.020
The k ratio describes the typical behavior in a random ensemble of graphs.

15:03.440 --> 15:05.920
It assumes connections are made somewhat independently.

15:06.400 --> 15:08.040
Oh, and the islands aren't independent.

15:08.440 --> 15:08.780
Right.

15:09.280 --> 15:13.340
In this island example, the graph is perfectly unnaturally clustered.

15:13.680 --> 15:14.580
It's highly atypical.

15:14.580 --> 15:16.980
The author uses this to prove a limitation.

15:17.920 --> 15:22.860
k measures the propensity of a network to branch out, but it absolutely does not guarantee

15:22.860 --> 15:27.880
that the specific, heavily clustered code sitting on your laptop is actually a giant

15:27.880 --> 15:28.400
superconduct.

15:28.400 --> 15:33.800
Which brings up a fascinating distinction the paper makes about threat models, because, you

15:33.800 --> 15:39.320
know, if we are looking for how things connect, traditional network theorists and cyber attackers,

15:39.540 --> 15:41.340
they actually want completely different things.

15:41.480 --> 15:41.820
Very true.

15:42.700 --> 15:46.620
You know, network theorists, they often look for what they call strong connectivity, and

15:46.620 --> 15:50.480
that's like measured by a direct, let's see, like a directed version of the ratio.

15:50.480 --> 15:52.300
So it's like k-dir.

15:53.140 --> 15:57.560
When k-dir is greater than one, you expect a giant strongly connected component.

15:57.560 --> 16:02.920
And strongly connected in directed graphs means that not only can you get from point A to

16:02.920 --> 16:07.380
point B following the arrows, but there is also a path following the arrows to get from

16:07.380 --> 16:09.220
point B back to point A.

16:09.460 --> 16:09.760
Right.

16:10.080 --> 16:12.000
I was thinking about this like a water system.

16:12.900 --> 16:16.120
Traditional network theorists are essentially looking for a whirlpool.

16:16.120 --> 16:21.160
They want a system where the water can circle endlessly, flowing from the top down to the

16:21.160 --> 16:23.560
bottom, and being pumped right back up to the top.

16:23.800 --> 16:24.340
That's a good visual.

16:24.920 --> 16:30.220
But if you are a hacker executing a supply chain attack, you do not need a whirlpool.

16:30.760 --> 16:33.140
You just need a water tower and a drain.

16:33.340 --> 16:35.160
That is a highly accurate way to frame it.

16:35.380 --> 16:37.800
You just need gravity to pull the water down one time.

16:38.220 --> 16:38.660
Exactly.

16:39.360 --> 16:45.120
An attacker just needs a pure source, say a maliciously injected dependency at the very top

16:45.120 --> 16:50.200
of the software chain, to reach a pure sync, which is your sensitive operation, like a

16:50.200 --> 16:52.120
database deletion command at the bottom.

16:52.500 --> 16:53.300
They don't need to go backwards.

16:53.600 --> 16:53.800
Right.

16:54.040 --> 16:57.560
The code execution doesn't need to flow back up to the malicious package.

16:58.200 --> 17:03.200
Therefore, having a k-dir greater than one is not a prerequisite for a massive compromise.

17:03.820 --> 17:09.260
A directed chain of a thousand packages has a k-dir of zero because nothing flows backward.

17:09.860 --> 17:13.980
But a single attack at the start can reach all 1,000 downstream targets.

17:13.980 --> 17:16.080
The operational threat isn't a loop.

17:16.420 --> 17:18.040
It's a one-way street of damage.

17:18.300 --> 17:20.820
But this raises a methodological problem for the author.

17:21.280 --> 17:21.600
What's that?

17:21.980 --> 17:27.180
Well, we can measure a software graph, find it has heavy clustering or a high k-ratio.

17:27.560 --> 17:30.860
But is that actually evidence of a vulnerable malicious structure?

17:31.080 --> 17:34.340
Or is that just what natural human-written software looks like?

17:34.500 --> 17:35.160
Oh, interesting.

17:35.160 --> 17:40.600
To prove that the vulnerabilities we find are structurally significant, we have to create

17:40.600 --> 17:41.240
a control group.

17:41.560 --> 17:45.040
We need a null ensemble to compare reality against.

17:45.360 --> 17:48.400
Let's get into the mechanics of this control group.

17:48.940 --> 17:54.240
How do you create a randomized baseline for something as complex as a software architecture?

17:54.900 --> 17:59.560
The paper proposes creating random graphs that act as our null hypothesis.

18:00.060 --> 18:01.900
But they have to be fair comparisons.

18:01.900 --> 18:03.740
You can't just throw dots on a page.

18:04.220 --> 18:04.240
Right.

18:04.320 --> 18:05.420
That wouldn't mean anything.

18:05.580 --> 18:05.880
Exactly.

18:06.420 --> 18:11.500
The random graphs must perfectly preserve three strict properties from the original software.

18:12.220 --> 18:17.080
The in-degree of every vertex, the out-degree of every vertex, and the package labels.

18:17.320 --> 18:22.900
Meaning if my calculate-tax function originally had two functions calling it and it called five

18:22.900 --> 18:28.240
other functions, it must keep exactly two incoming lines and five outgoing lines in the randomized

18:28.240 --> 18:28.740
version.

18:28.740 --> 18:29.820
Yes, exactly.

18:30.340 --> 18:35.760
It preserves the local traffic volume of every single piece of code, but it completely scrambles

18:35.760 --> 18:36.760
who is talking to whom.

18:37.000 --> 18:37.400
Got it.

18:37.660 --> 18:42.600
And to do this scrambling, the author uses a technique called directed double-edged swaps.

18:43.020 --> 18:45.520
Let me make sure I understand the mechanics of the swap.

18:46.040 --> 18:48.020
Why a double-edged swap?

18:48.140 --> 18:50.100
Why not just pick one line and move its endpoint?

18:50.620 --> 18:54.380
Because if you just take a line pointing from A to B and you move its sort of points from A

18:54.380 --> 18:56.560
to C, you just ruin the degrees.

18:57.180 --> 18:57.760
Oh, I see.

18:57.760 --> 19:00.640
B just lost an incoming connection, and C just gained one.

19:01.360 --> 19:04.880
Your control group no longer matches the original software's local traffic rules.

19:05.260 --> 19:05.920
Oh, okay.

19:06.140 --> 19:07.080
So you have to swap pairs.

19:07.700 --> 19:12.220
Let's say connection one goes from point A to point B. Connection two goes from point

19:12.220 --> 19:16.540
C to point D. To scramble them without changing the totals, you swap the destinations.

19:17.360 --> 19:19.880
So now A points to D and C points to B.

19:20.060 --> 19:20.380
Exactly.

19:20.780 --> 19:24.040
Every point still has the exact same number of arrows leaving and entering.

19:24.040 --> 19:24.960
Precisely.

19:25.420 --> 19:31.180
As long as doing that swap doesn't create an illegal self-loop, like a function calling itself when it didn't before,

19:31.560 --> 19:33.700
or a duplicate edge, you make the swap.

19:34.160 --> 19:40.600
By executing this double swap thousands of times, you create a deeply randomized version of your software graph

19:40.600 --> 19:43.340
that still obeys all the macro rules of the original.

19:43.340 --> 19:46.200
But the author hits a mathematical wall here.

19:46.720 --> 19:50.960
Something called a disconnected state space, or irreducibility.

19:51.520 --> 19:56.520
This is where it gets really deep into the weeds, but the implications for researchers are huge.

19:57.100 --> 19:59.740
What exactly causes the state space to disconnect?

19:59.740 --> 20:05.660
It boils down to the fact that just because you are randomly swapping edges doesn't mean you can

20:05.660 --> 20:08.400
mathematically reach every possible valid graph shape.

20:09.100 --> 20:11.000
You can get trapped in a pocket of possibilities.

20:11.720 --> 20:12.020
How?

20:12.280 --> 20:16.480
If you can swap any two edges, shouldn't you eventually be able to shuffle into any combination?

20:16.860 --> 20:18.340
Not with the constraints we have.

20:18.960 --> 20:20.780
The paper gives a minimalist example.

20:21.040 --> 20:23.860
Imagine just three dots, vertices 1, 2, and 3.

20:23.860 --> 20:28.980
The rule is that each dot must have exactly one incoming edge and one outgoing edge.

20:29.540 --> 20:33.280
The only way to satisfy that rule is a directed cycle, a triangle.

20:33.820 --> 20:37.540
One points to two, two points to three, and three points to one.

20:37.700 --> 20:38.800
Okay, a closed loop.

20:39.240 --> 20:44.100
Now, there are two valid ways that triangle can flow, clockwise or counterclockwise.

20:44.700 --> 20:48.920
Both are perfectly valid configurations with the exact same in and out degrees.

20:49.080 --> 20:49.400
Right.

20:49.400 --> 20:54.560
But if you start with the clockwise triangle and you try to use a double-edged swap to randomize it,

20:54.920 --> 20:56.020
you literally can't.

20:56.540 --> 20:58.040
Wait, let me think about that.

20:58.400 --> 21:04.560
If I take the edge from 1 to 2 and the edge from 2 to 3 and I try to swap their destinations,

21:04.980 --> 21:05.100
Right.

21:05.560 --> 21:08.700
1 would point to 3, but 2 would point to 2.

21:08.940 --> 21:09.360
Exactly.

21:09.880 --> 21:12.120
You instantly create an illegal self-loop.

21:12.900 --> 21:16.740
Any valid swap you attempt between two edges in that triangle will break the rules.

21:17.060 --> 21:18.400
You are stuck.

21:18.400 --> 21:19.240
Oh, wow.

21:19.400 --> 21:23.920
You could never randomly swap your way from the clockwise triangle to the counterclockwise triangle.

21:24.560 --> 21:28.580
The state space of all possible graphs is physically disconnected.

21:28.960 --> 21:35.220
So, if the randomizer gets stuck in one isolated corner of the universe and can't explore all the possibilities,

21:35.740 --> 21:37.420
how can we trust the comparison?

21:37.600 --> 21:41.420
I mean, it's like trying to calculate the average temperature of the Earth,

21:41.540 --> 21:44.460
but your thermometer is physically locked inside a walk-in freezer.

21:44.940 --> 21:46.980
The control group is inherently biased.

21:46.980 --> 21:50.760
This is a major issue in Markov chain Monte Carlo simulations.

21:50.760 --> 21:58.640
Membership in a null ensemble doesn't magically guarantee that your samples are statistically fair or exchangeable.

21:58.860 --> 21:59.820
So how do they fix it?

22:00.220 --> 22:05.980
To solve this, the author implements a method developed by Bezag and Clifford called the pivot construction.

22:06.380 --> 22:07.540
I read this part a few times.

22:07.540 --> 22:09.180
Can we use an analogy here?

22:09.600 --> 22:10.660
I was trying to map it out.

22:10.800 --> 22:12.740
It's like shuffling a deck of cards, right?

22:13.260 --> 22:14.180
Let's run with that.

22:14.860 --> 22:17.800
How does the card shuffle map to the state space?

22:17.800 --> 22:22.020
Well, if you only swap two cards once, you haven't shuffled the deck.

22:22.380 --> 22:24.960
The deck is heavily biased by whatever order it started in.

22:25.460 --> 22:29.420
You are stuck in a disconnected state relative to a truly random deck.

22:29.580 --> 22:29.700
Right.

22:30.160 --> 22:34.180
If you deal hands from that deck and compare them to my perfectly randomized deck,

22:34.660 --> 22:38.580
the statistical comparison is totally invalid because our origins were different.

22:38.780 --> 22:39.640
Yes, exactly.

22:39.640 --> 22:48.400
So the pivot construction is the mathematical equivalent of giving the deck a massive standardized riffle shuffle before you deal the hands.

22:49.120 --> 22:54.420
Instead of comparing your observed software graph to a bunch of random graphs generated from scratch,

22:54.660 --> 22:59.820
you take your observed graph and you swap edges for a huge number of steps.

22:59.960 --> 23:01.240
Let's call it L steps.

23:01.500 --> 23:02.760
Right. You thoroughly shuffle.

23:02.900 --> 23:06.120
You thoroughly shuffle the deck to a new randomized state.

23:06.860 --> 23:08.540
This new state is the pivot.

23:08.540 --> 23:09.900
That is the crucial step.

23:10.520 --> 23:11.980
The pivot is the newly shuffled deck.

23:12.680 --> 23:20.800
From that exact pivot deck, you then run multiple independent chains of further shuffling to create your B comparison graphs.

23:21.260 --> 23:24.960
Because both your original graph, traced backward from the pivot,

23:25.520 --> 23:28.840
and your simulated graphs, traced forward from the pivot,

23:29.560 --> 23:33.880
can map their lineage back to this shared, reversible pivot point,

23:34.460 --> 23:36.680
every hand dealt is mathematically fair.

23:36.680 --> 23:38.340
It's a closed loop of fairness.

23:38.540 --> 23:38.900
Yeah.

23:39.360 --> 23:42.320
It ensures your statistical comparison is actually valid,

23:42.820 --> 23:46.680
even if the entire universe of possible card combinations hasn't been explored.

23:46.680 --> 23:50.700
That is a phenomenal way to explain the pivot construction.

23:51.360 --> 23:55.080
It forces statistical exchangeability under the null hypothesis.

23:55.500 --> 23:57.600
It grounds the comparison in rigorous fairness.

23:57.600 --> 24:01.680
So when we say this software is unusually vulnerable,

24:01.880 --> 24:05.740
we know it's a mathematical fact, not an artifact of a bad randomizer.

24:05.740 --> 24:10.080
Okay, so our null model proves our software's connectivity isn't a fluke.

24:10.080 --> 24:13.320
We know the system is structurally vulnerable at a macro level.

24:13.960 --> 24:16.720
But how does an attacker actually exploit that?

24:16.940 --> 24:20.260
Moving from macroscopic statistics to microscopic reality,

24:20.500 --> 24:24.700
how do we find the specific microscopic path an attacker might take?

24:24.700 --> 24:29.060
This is where the paper shifts to finding actual witnesses and choke points.

24:29.260 --> 24:29.400
Right.

24:30.240 --> 24:32.040
Here's where it gets really interesting.

24:32.780 --> 24:33.720
Let's define the terminology.

24:34.360 --> 24:39.080
Finding a path on the graph simply means tracing a labeled walk from an entry vertex,

24:39.420 --> 24:41.640
the specific function where an attacker injects their code,

24:41.920 --> 24:45.580
to a sensitive set, the specific operation they want to hijack.

24:45.580 --> 24:49.040
But the author throws a massive bucket of cold water on this right away.

24:49.780 --> 24:51.860
They emphasize a crucial caveat.

24:52.760 --> 24:56.460
A graph path is only a candidate capability path.

24:56.960 --> 24:57.460
By candidate.

24:57.760 --> 25:01.740
This might be the most important practical takeaway for a developer listening to this.

25:02.440 --> 25:06.120
Just because a line exists on paper or on your full graph map

25:06.120 --> 25:09.560
does not mean it can actually execute in the real world.

25:10.340 --> 25:12.640
A graph witness is just structural proof.

25:13.040 --> 25:14.820
I want to dig into how that happens.

25:14.820 --> 25:18.880
Because if the code literally says function A calls function B,

25:19.340 --> 25:20.220
doesn't it call it?

25:20.640 --> 25:21.900
How can the map be wrong?

25:22.440 --> 25:23.460
The map isn't wrong.

25:23.860 --> 25:25.060
It's just static.

25:25.700 --> 25:28.300
It lacks the dimension of time and state.

25:29.360 --> 25:29.700
Oh.

25:30.100 --> 25:33.380
A sequence of calls might look perfectly connected structurally.

25:33.720 --> 25:37.440
But at runtime, it might require incompatible branch conditions.

25:38.300 --> 25:42.140
For example, function A might only call function B

25:42.140 --> 25:45.900
if a specific user role variable is set to admin.

25:46.480 --> 25:47.040
Right.

25:47.040 --> 25:49.600
But to reach function A in the first place,

25:50.140 --> 25:53.320
maybe the attacker had to exploit a vulnerability in a public portal

25:53.320 --> 25:56.520
that forcibly sets the user role to guest.

25:56.520 --> 26:04.100
So structurally, the path from the portal to A to B exists on the map.

26:04.340 --> 26:09.260
But functionally, it is impossible to traverse because the required variables contradict each other.

26:09.980 --> 26:10.420
Exactly.

26:11.080 --> 26:13.040
You also have conflicting file permissions,

26:13.600 --> 26:14.420
hardware constraints,

26:14.420 --> 26:18.740
or specific runtime states that the static parser simply cannot see.

26:19.780 --> 26:21.580
So a graph witness is basically saying,

26:22.120 --> 26:25.140
hey, there is a physical road between these two cities,

26:25.300 --> 26:28.380
but it doesn't tell you if there is a massive steel gate

26:28.380 --> 26:30.280
locked across the road halfway down

26:30.280 --> 26:32.980
or if the bridge is currently drawn up.

26:33.160 --> 26:34.620
It's a candidate path.

26:35.200 --> 26:35.520
Yes.

26:35.520 --> 26:39.120
The graph tells you exactly where to aim your magnifying glass,

26:39.300 --> 26:43.420
but a human or a dynamic execution engine still has to investigate

26:43.420 --> 26:45.880
to see if it's a truly executable exploit.

26:46.300 --> 26:46.540
Yes.

26:46.940 --> 26:49.200
And when we do map these candidate attack routes,

26:49.640 --> 26:52.180
we need a vocabulary to describe the robustness.

26:52.600 --> 26:55.320
How hard is it for a defender to block the attack?

26:55.680 --> 26:57.820
They use a concept called disjointness.

26:58.420 --> 26:59.680
Let's get into disjointness,

26:59.880 --> 27:01.920
because the paper splits it into two flavors,

27:02.320 --> 27:05.320
edge disjoint and vertex disjoint paths.

27:05.520 --> 27:07.120
What is the mechanical difference?

27:07.660 --> 27:10.120
Think of the entry point and the sensitive target.

27:10.660 --> 27:13.120
How many distinct ways can the attacker get there?

27:13.460 --> 27:17.000
If you have multiple paths that don't share a single edge,

27:17.400 --> 27:19.400
meaning they don't use any of the same connections,

27:19.900 --> 27:21.720
those are edge disjoint paths.

27:22.160 --> 27:23.420
But there's a trap there, right?

27:23.720 --> 27:24.440
A huge trap.

27:24.920 --> 27:27.080
Two paths might use completely different edges,

27:27.080 --> 27:30.480
but they might both pass through the exact same intermediate vertex.

27:31.120 --> 27:34.060
Say they both utilize a shared utility function,

27:34.060 --> 27:36.060
like a universal string formatter.

27:36.380 --> 27:36.500
Okay.

27:36.760 --> 27:39.780
If that string formatter goes down, both paths break.

27:40.280 --> 27:42.680
If you have paths that share absolutely nothing,

27:42.880 --> 27:45.360
no edges and no intermediate vertices,

27:45.740 --> 27:47.560
those are vertex disjoint paths.

27:47.960 --> 27:50.880
Which brings us to the holy grail of defensive analysis,

27:51.540 --> 27:52.580
the essential vertices.

27:52.580 --> 27:56.340
An essential vertex is a specific piece of code

27:56.340 --> 27:57.800
that, if you remove it,

27:58.000 --> 28:00.320
completely eliminates every single route

28:00.320 --> 28:02.580
from the entry to the sensitive set.

28:03.140 --> 28:04.640
It is the ultimate choke point.

28:04.880 --> 28:05.100
Right.

28:05.520 --> 28:06.560
If you are a defender,

28:06.860 --> 28:08.760
finding an essential vertex is gold.

28:08.940 --> 28:11.520
It tells you exactly where to focus your patching efforts.

28:11.920 --> 28:13.700
It is gold for triaging, absolutely.

28:14.260 --> 28:15.980
But the author specifically warns

28:15.980 --> 28:18.180
against a naive approach to essential vertices.

28:18.900 --> 28:20.940
Identifying the choke point is just step one.

28:21.520 --> 28:23.980
You cannot just delete the essential vertex

28:23.980 --> 28:25.520
and declare the system secure.

28:25.860 --> 28:26.380
Right.

28:26.480 --> 28:27.380
I was thinking about this.

28:27.500 --> 28:30.440
What if the essential vertex is the core function

28:30.440 --> 28:31.900
that authenticates users

28:31.900 --> 28:33.880
or saves data to the hard drive?

28:34.020 --> 28:34.300
Exactly.

28:34.500 --> 28:35.460
If you just delete it,

28:35.780 --> 28:37.340
sure, you stopped the hacker's path,

28:37.440 --> 28:39.740
but you also bricked the entire software

28:39.740 --> 28:40.800
for legitimate users.

28:41.560 --> 28:42.920
Fixing a choke point usually means

28:42.920 --> 28:44.100
adding sanitization

28:44.100 --> 28:46.820
or permission checks inside that vertex,

28:46.820 --> 28:48.660
not just ripping it out of the wall.

28:49.240 --> 28:50.620
And to truly understand the difference

28:50.620 --> 28:52.260
between simply finding a path

28:52.260 --> 28:54.140
and understanding how vulnerable

28:54.140 --> 28:55.960
that path actually makes your system,

28:56.620 --> 28:59.280
the author takes a brilliant interdisciplinary leap.

28:59.700 --> 29:02.080
They borrow an analogy from material science,

29:02.440 --> 29:05.020
specifically the physics of porous batteries.

29:05.760 --> 29:06.360
I have to say,

29:06.440 --> 29:07.640
when I was reading the source notes

29:07.640 --> 29:09.700
and suddenly we were deep in the weeds

29:09.700 --> 29:11.240
of 3D porous electrodes,

29:11.460 --> 29:13.000
I was a bit lost.

29:13.000 --> 29:15.560
But when you unpack the mechanics of it,

29:15.620 --> 29:17.400
it is a phenomenal crossover insight.

29:17.900 --> 29:18.920
It requires some translation,

29:19.280 --> 29:21.920
but the physics map perfectly to software security.

29:22.600 --> 29:25.160
The author cites a study by Rodriguez et al.,

29:25.160 --> 29:26.800
which looked at the microstructures

29:26.800 --> 29:29.040
of four different 3D porous electrodes.

29:29.380 --> 29:30.180
So, batteries.

29:30.500 --> 29:30.700
Right.

29:31.060 --> 29:33.480
These are basically microscopic sponges

29:33.480 --> 29:34.680
made of conducting material.

29:35.540 --> 29:36.680
The researchers wanted to see

29:36.680 --> 29:38.020
how well they conducted electricity

29:38.020 --> 29:40.300
from one face of the sponge to the other.

29:40.300 --> 29:41.920
And the first thing they measured

29:41.920 --> 29:44.100
was something called spanning connectivity.

29:44.580 --> 29:44.700
Right.

29:45.480 --> 29:47.820
Spanning connectivity asks a binary question.

29:48.660 --> 29:50.680
Is there an unbroken physical path

29:50.680 --> 29:51.720
of conducting material

29:51.720 --> 29:54.020
from the inlet face to the outlet face?

29:54.200 --> 29:54.880
Like a flood fill.

29:55.120 --> 29:55.360
Yes.

29:55.720 --> 29:57.520
If you were to do a flood fill algorithm,

29:57.640 --> 29:59.440
like the paint bucket tool in Photoshop,

29:59.740 --> 30:00.660
starting at one side,

30:01.000 --> 30:02.420
would the paint eventually bleed through

30:02.420 --> 30:03.080
to the other side?

30:03.380 --> 30:04.400
And the data they got back

30:04.400 --> 30:06.000
was incredibly counterintuitive.

30:06.420 --> 30:08.020
They simulated four different

30:08.020 --> 30:09.600
microscopic sponge structures,

30:09.600 --> 30:11.360
all four of them successfully

30:11.360 --> 30:12.160
spanned the volume.

30:12.720 --> 30:14.300
The fractions of conducting material

30:14.300 --> 30:15.720
that spanned from side to side

30:15.720 --> 30:16.700
were almost identical,

30:16.960 --> 30:21.660
0.996, 0.999, 0.997, and 0.901.

30:21.760 --> 30:22.480
Very close.

30:22.840 --> 30:23.580
So, structurally,

30:23.740 --> 30:25.440
they all looked like nearly perfect,

30:25.560 --> 30:26.820
identical conductors.

30:27.240 --> 30:28.260
But then they simulated

30:28.260 --> 30:30.300
applying a voltage across the faces

30:30.300 --> 30:31.260
to measure the actual

30:31.260 --> 30:32.400
effective conductivity,

30:32.840 --> 30:34.260
how well electricity actually

30:34.260 --> 30:35.380
flowed through them in practice.

30:36.020 --> 30:37.880
And the conductivity varied wildly.

30:37.880 --> 30:40.200
Let me look at the numbers.

30:40.720 --> 30:42.260
The normalized conductivities were

30:42.260 --> 30:49.000
0.306, 0.303, 0.706, and 0.134.

30:49.400 --> 30:50.440
Wait, let me look at that.

30:51.200 --> 30:53.420
Three of those volumes had spanning sizes

30:53.420 --> 30:55.680
within three one thousandths of each other,

30:55.780 --> 30:57.840
0.996, 0.997, 0.999.

30:58.320 --> 31:01.220
They looked identical to the spanning metric.

31:01.440 --> 31:01.660
Yep.

31:01.660 --> 31:04.220
Yet their actual physical conductivity

31:04.220 --> 31:08.640
was 0.306, 0.303, and 0.706.

31:09.300 --> 31:10.820
The third one conducted electricity

31:10.820 --> 31:12.940
more than twice as well as the others.

31:13.480 --> 31:13.860
Exactly.

31:14.260 --> 31:15.900
Let's translate this physics lesson

31:15.900 --> 31:17.100
back into software security.

31:17.860 --> 31:18.800
Spanning a volume,

31:19.380 --> 31:20.480
doing that flood fill

31:20.480 --> 31:21.740
to see if electricity can get

31:21.740 --> 31:22.920
from the inlet to the outlet,

31:23.440 --> 31:24.880
is exactly the same concept

31:24.880 --> 31:26.220
as finding a witness path

31:26.220 --> 31:27.200
in a software graph.

31:27.640 --> 31:28.380
Okay, I'm tracking.

31:28.380 --> 31:30.980
It just proves that a structural connection

31:30.980 --> 31:33.220
exists from the attacker's entry point

31:33.220 --> 31:34.300
to the sensitive data.

31:35.080 --> 31:36.720
So a standard security scanner

31:36.720 --> 31:38.740
that just says warning, path found,

31:38.940 --> 31:41.300
is only measuring spanning connectivity.

31:41.840 --> 31:42.340
Precisely.

31:42.980 --> 31:44.420
And the conductivity of the battery

31:44.420 --> 31:46.040
is the security equivalent

31:46.040 --> 31:48.080
of the robustness of the attack.

31:48.680 --> 31:49.920
Oh, that makes so much sense.

31:50.540 --> 31:52.080
How many independent routes exist?

31:52.540 --> 31:54.120
What is the minimum cut,

31:54.400 --> 31:55.600
the minimum number of edges

31:55.600 --> 31:56.440
you'd have to sever

31:56.440 --> 31:57.720
to break the connection?

31:57.720 --> 31:59.320
How many choke points are there?

31:59.940 --> 32:00.880
It's the difference

32:00.880 --> 32:02.000
between a four-lane highway

32:02.000 --> 32:03.700
and a rickety wooden footbridge.

32:04.100 --> 32:05.900
They both span the river.

32:06.140 --> 32:07.800
If you just ask, can it cross?

32:08.000 --> 32:09.620
The answer is yes for both,

32:09.900 --> 32:11.220
but they conduct traffic

32:11.220 --> 32:11.940
very differently.

32:12.400 --> 32:12.800
Exactly.

32:13.280 --> 32:15.000
If a software path relies

32:15.000 --> 32:17.100
on a single, fragile choke point,

32:17.320 --> 32:18.600
it might span the system,

32:18.820 --> 32:19.920
but its conductivity

32:19.920 --> 32:21.660
for an attacker is very low.

32:21.880 --> 32:23.300
It's cheap to remediate.

32:23.400 --> 32:24.800
You just guard that one bridge.

32:24.800 --> 32:26.880
But a system with multiple

32:26.880 --> 32:28.820
vertex disjoint attack routes

32:28.820 --> 32:30.160
has high conductivity.

32:30.700 --> 32:31.740
It is highly vulnerable,

32:32.180 --> 32:33.420
even if the sheer length

32:33.420 --> 32:33.980
of the paths

32:33.980 --> 32:35.460
looks identical on paper.

32:36.080 --> 32:38.000
Now, academic rigor requires us

32:38.000 --> 32:38.960
to point out the flaws,

32:39.300 --> 32:40.520
and the author responsibly

32:40.520 --> 32:41.480
applies the brakes here.

32:42.280 --> 32:43.140
They issue cautions

32:43.140 --> 32:44.180
about fully relying

32:44.180 --> 32:45.300
on the quantitative math

32:45.300 --> 32:46.360
of that battery study.

32:46.700 --> 32:47.400
Right, because it wasn't

32:47.400 --> 32:48.040
a perfect study.

32:48.260 --> 32:48.860
No, it wasn't.

32:48.860 --> 32:50.180
The author notes

32:50.180 --> 32:51.320
that the Rodriguez study

32:51.320 --> 32:53.020
lacked experimental validation.

32:53.440 --> 32:54.480
It was purely simulated

32:54.480 --> 32:55.140
on a computer.

32:56.060 --> 32:56.300
Furthermore,

32:56.560 --> 32:57.660
the massive conclusion

32:57.660 --> 32:58.520
about the variance

32:58.520 --> 32:59.280
in conductivity

32:59.280 --> 33:01.640
was based on a Pearson correlation

33:01.640 --> 33:03.320
of only four data points.

33:03.580 --> 33:04.040
Only four?

33:04.260 --> 33:04.480
Yeah.

33:05.240 --> 33:06.040
Four microstructures

33:06.040 --> 33:08.240
is a tiny sample size

33:08.240 --> 33:10.540
to build a generalized law upon.

33:11.040 --> 33:12.100
The author also mentions

33:12.100 --> 33:13.040
that the battery paper

33:13.040 --> 33:13.660
had confusing

33:13.660 --> 33:15.020
and contradictory definitions

33:15.020 --> 33:16.640
for its own bottleneck index.

33:17.000 --> 33:17.300
Okay.

33:17.300 --> 33:19.720
So, while the qualitative analogy

33:19.720 --> 33:20.420
is brilliant

33:20.420 --> 33:22.100
for changing how we think

33:22.100 --> 33:23.460
about software robustness,

33:23.860 --> 33:25.540
the actual mathematical formulas

33:25.540 --> 33:26.660
from the battery physics

33:26.660 --> 33:28.480
shouldn't be blindly pasted

33:28.480 --> 33:29.500
into network security.

33:29.940 --> 33:30.820
That's totally fair.

33:31.040 --> 33:32.520
It's a conceptual framework,

33:32.660 --> 33:34.200
not a plug-and-play algorithm.

33:34.620 --> 33:35.600
But speaking of testing

33:35.600 --> 33:36.500
mathematical theories,

33:36.920 --> 33:37.700
we've talked about

33:37.700 --> 33:38.980
a lot of high-level math,

33:39.380 --> 33:40.040
K-ratios,

33:40.220 --> 33:41.100
pivot constructions,

33:41.300 --> 33:41.880
minimum cuts.

33:42.540 --> 33:43.180
How do we know

33:43.180 --> 33:44.320
any of this actually works?

33:44.760 --> 33:45.680
We have to test it.

33:45.680 --> 33:46.060
Right.

33:46.060 --> 33:47.060
That brings us

33:47.060 --> 33:47.900
to a crucial part

33:47.900 --> 33:48.320
of the paper

33:48.320 --> 33:49.060
where the author

33:49.060 --> 33:50.380
rigorously stress-tests

33:50.380 --> 33:50.860
these metrics

33:50.860 --> 33:52.120
in a synthetic sandbox.

33:52.800 --> 33:53.520
And they didn't just use

33:53.520 --> 33:54.920
standard random graphs

33:54.920 --> 33:55.660
for this sandbox.

33:56.020 --> 33:57.620
They used the Chung-Loo model.

33:58.200 --> 33:58.780
What is that?

33:59.140 --> 34:00.380
As we discussed earlier,

34:00.560 --> 34:02.080
standard Erdos-Renyi graphs

34:02.080 --> 34:03.440
produce bell curves,

34:03.860 --> 34:05.040
which don't look like software.

34:05.600 --> 34:07.080
The Chung-Loo model

34:07.080 --> 34:08.140
allows researchers

34:08.140 --> 34:09.580
to generate random graphs

34:09.580 --> 34:11.020
that precisely match

34:11.020 --> 34:12.480
the heavy-tailed

34:12.480 --> 34:14.240
power law degree distributions

34:14.240 --> 34:15.200
we actually see

34:15.200 --> 34:16.280
in software ecosystems.

34:16.860 --> 34:18.000
They force the randomizer

34:18.000 --> 34:19.000
to create hubs.

34:19.620 --> 34:20.140
The author ran

34:20.140 --> 34:21.160
four key experiments

34:21.160 --> 34:22.700
in this synthetic sandbox,

34:23.000 --> 34:23.820
and I want to look at them

34:23.820 --> 34:24.840
as a unified narrative,

34:25.020 --> 34:25.140
you know,

34:25.180 --> 34:25.920
a quest to see if

34:25.920 --> 34:27.000
the math survives

34:27.000 --> 34:28.080
contact with reality.

34:28.640 --> 34:29.080
First,

34:29.180 --> 34:29.720
they had to prove

34:29.720 --> 34:30.500
the thresholds

34:30.500 --> 34:31.340
weren't just theoretical.

34:31.680 --> 34:32.260
They generated

34:32.260 --> 34:33.360
a network of

34:33.360 --> 34:34.320
3,000 vertices,

34:34.720 --> 34:35.740
3,000 simulated

34:35.740 --> 34:36.580
pieces of code,

34:36.700 --> 34:38.240
and watched what happened

34:38.240 --> 34:39.100
as they cranked up

34:39.100 --> 34:39.620
the connectivity.

34:40.180 --> 34:40.440
Right.

34:40.440 --> 34:42.100
What did the data show

34:42.100 --> 34:42.600
about the

34:42.600 --> 34:43.920
Molloy-Reed-K ratio?

34:44.640 --> 34:45.600
It proved the theory,

34:45.980 --> 34:47.900
but with a vivid reality check.

34:48.380 --> 34:49.300
As the network

34:49.300 --> 34:50.420
became more connected,

34:50.920 --> 34:52.160
the K ratio grew.

34:52.920 --> 34:53.920
When K went from

34:53.920 --> 34:56.800
2.77 to 5.76,

34:57.580 --> 34:58.840
the size of the largest

34:58.840 --> 35:00.180
weakly connected component,

35:00.560 --> 35:02.360
the giant continent of code,

35:02.940 --> 35:03.780
jumped from holding

35:03.780 --> 35:05.780
53.9% of the graph

35:05.780 --> 35:06.900
to a staggering

35:06.900 --> 35:09.160
93.9% of the graph.

35:09.160 --> 35:10.520
So it proves that

35:10.520 --> 35:12.000
the ratio is highly accurate

35:12.000 --> 35:13.100
at marking the onset

35:13.100 --> 35:13.860
of the tipping point.

35:14.220 --> 35:15.300
The moment K gets high,

35:15.460 --> 35:16.400
the graph coalesces

35:16.400 --> 35:17.200
into a monolith.

35:17.780 --> 35:18.160
Yes,

35:18.500 --> 35:19.400
but it also confirmed

35:19.400 --> 35:20.640
the limitation we discussed

35:20.640 --> 35:22.040
with the archipelago example.

35:22.800 --> 35:24.260
The ratio marks the onset,

35:24.880 --> 35:25.740
but it doesn't predict

35:25.740 --> 35:26.680
the exact size

35:26.680 --> 35:27.960
or shape of the component.

35:28.540 --> 35:29.360
It just tells you

35:29.360 --> 35:30.140
that you're operating

35:30.140 --> 35:30.860
in a regime

35:30.860 --> 35:32.520
where massive connectivity

35:32.520 --> 35:33.120
is the norm.

35:33.600 --> 35:33.940
Okay,

35:34.180 --> 35:35.680
so the threshold theory

35:35.680 --> 35:36.240
holds up.

35:36.940 --> 35:37.280
Next,

35:37.380 --> 35:37.980
they had to test

35:37.980 --> 35:39.300
their control group methodology.

35:40.000 --> 35:42.180
This was the irreducibility test.

35:42.620 --> 35:43.180
This goes back

35:43.180 --> 35:44.440
to our card shuffling analogy

35:44.440 --> 35:45.700
with the directed

35:45.700 --> 35:46.900
double edge swaps.

35:47.180 --> 35:47.620
Yes,

35:47.760 --> 35:49.140
the six vertex sandbox.

35:49.600 --> 35:49.820
Right,

35:49.920 --> 35:50.540
they tested this

35:50.540 --> 35:51.580
on a tiny sandbox

35:51.580 --> 35:52.820
of just six vertices

35:52.820 --> 35:54.040
to see how often

35:54.040 --> 35:54.740
the randomizer

35:54.740 --> 35:55.320
got stuck

35:55.320 --> 35:56.840
in a disconnected state space.

35:57.040 --> 35:57.880
And the results

35:57.880 --> 35:58.860
were stark.

35:59.540 --> 36:00.020
They found that

36:00.020 --> 36:01.400
even on a tiny graph,

36:01.780 --> 36:02.380
if you used

36:02.380 --> 36:03.480
unrestricted swaps,

36:03.840 --> 36:04.660
the state space

36:04.660 --> 36:05.300
was disconnected

36:05.300 --> 36:06.600
and up to 39%

36:06.600 --> 36:07.680
of the classes tested.

36:08.260 --> 36:09.040
The randomizer

36:09.040 --> 36:09.640
got trapped

36:09.640 --> 36:11.440
39% of the time.

36:11.880 --> 36:12.220
Wow.

36:12.440 --> 36:13.220
But it gets worse.

36:13.740 --> 36:14.520
They also tested

36:14.520 --> 36:15.760
a stricter swap method

36:15.760 --> 36:16.880
called Variant B,

36:17.300 --> 36:18.060
which forced swaps

36:18.060 --> 36:18.700
to only happen

36:18.700 --> 36:19.480
between the exact

36:19.480 --> 36:20.760
same pair of packages,

36:21.260 --> 36:21.940
trying to preserve

36:21.940 --> 36:22.920
even more structure.

36:22.920 --> 36:24.360
How did Variant B perform?

36:25.120 --> 36:26.540
It failed catastrophically.

36:27.080 --> 36:27.800
It was disconnected

36:27.800 --> 36:29.380
95% of the time.

36:30.600 --> 36:31.200
95%.

36:31.200 --> 36:31.640
Yes.

36:31.640 --> 36:32.940
This proved

36:32.940 --> 36:33.500
beyond a shadow

36:33.500 --> 36:33.960
of a doubt

36:33.960 --> 36:35.320
that a reachable component

36:35.320 --> 36:36.040
is a real

36:36.040 --> 36:37.260
mathematical restriction.

36:37.860 --> 36:38.460
If you use

36:38.460 --> 36:39.400
the wrong null model

36:39.400 --> 36:40.680
or don't use

36:40.680 --> 36:41.520
the pivot construction

36:41.520 --> 36:42.380
to properly shuffle

36:42.380 --> 36:42.840
the deck,

36:43.280 --> 36:44.060
your control group

36:44.060 --> 36:44.680
is fundamentally

36:44.680 --> 36:46.420
broken 95%

36:46.420 --> 36:46.920
of the time.

36:47.560 --> 36:48.320
Any statistical

36:48.320 --> 36:49.280
conclusions you draw

36:49.280 --> 36:49.580
from it

36:49.580 --> 36:50.660
are basically garbage.

36:51.060 --> 36:52.220
That is a massive

36:52.220 --> 36:52.860
red flag

36:52.860 --> 36:53.780
for any research

36:53.780 --> 36:54.280
here doing

36:54.280 --> 36:55.360
statistical analysis

36:55.360 --> 36:56.100
on networks.

36:56.640 --> 36:57.340
You can't skip

36:57.340 --> 36:58.240
the rigorous math.

36:58.880 --> 36:59.020
Okay.

36:59.500 --> 37:00.800
So the threshold works,

37:00.800 --> 37:02.160
the control group

37:02.160 --> 37:03.200
requires the pivot

37:03.200 --> 37:03.780
construction.

37:04.620 --> 37:05.640
Now for the third test,

37:05.800 --> 37:07.000
which honestly sounds

37:07.000 --> 37:07.960
like a spy novel,

37:08.320 --> 37:09.980
detecting planted paths.

37:10.340 --> 37:11.000
My favorite part.

37:11.480 --> 37:12.820
The author purposefully

37:12.820 --> 37:14.560
hid a secret malicious path,

37:14.900 --> 37:16.020
four packages long,

37:16.420 --> 37:17.860
inside the synthetic graphs.

37:18.420 --> 37:19.020
Then they tested

37:19.020 --> 37:19.660
two different

37:19.660 --> 37:20.500
statistical methods

37:20.500 --> 37:21.320
to see which one

37:21.320 --> 37:22.300
could find the hidden threat.

37:22.540 --> 37:23.360
They wanted to compare

37:23.360 --> 37:24.460
path-level statistics

37:24.460 --> 37:25.800
against global statistics.

37:26.260 --> 37:27.120
They used the

37:27.120 --> 37:28.480
distance statistic,

37:28.480 --> 37:29.800
which looks for

37:29.800 --> 37:30.880
unusually short paths

37:30.880 --> 37:32.020
between specific points,

37:32.380 --> 37:33.260
and compared it

37:33.260 --> 37:33.720
against the

37:33.720 --> 37:35.320
forward-reach statistic,

37:35.560 --> 37:36.220
which just looks

37:36.220 --> 37:37.320
at the sheer volume

37:37.320 --> 37:37.940
of code

37:37.940 --> 37:38.880
an entry point

37:38.880 --> 37:39.940
can touch globally.

37:40.440 --> 37:40.860
Which one won?

37:41.720 --> 37:42.720
The distance statistic

37:42.720 --> 37:43.920
successfully detected

37:43.920 --> 37:44.660
the planted path

37:44.660 --> 37:46.380
with 85% power.

37:46.740 --> 37:48.180
It was highly effective

37:48.180 --> 37:49.220
at isolating the signal.

37:49.680 --> 37:51.520
And the forward-reach statistic?

37:52.020 --> 37:52.880
It completely failed.

37:53.360 --> 37:54.880
It only had a 7.5%

37:54.880 --> 37:56.260
power to detect the plant.

37:56.260 --> 37:58.360
Wait, why such a massive failure?

37:58.880 --> 38:00.060
It seems like if a package

38:00.060 --> 38:00.800
suddenly reaches

38:00.800 --> 38:01.700
a sensitive target,

38:01.900 --> 38:02.980
its forward-reach

38:02.980 --> 38:03.580
should spike.

38:04.080 --> 38:04.620
That would be true

38:04.620 --> 38:05.340
in a sparse network.

38:05.980 --> 38:07.260
But remember the first test.

38:07.660 --> 38:08.800
The graph was already

38:08.800 --> 38:09.780
operating in the

38:09.780 --> 38:10.880
supercritical regime.

38:11.140 --> 38:11.420
Oh.

38:11.820 --> 38:12.540
It was already past

38:12.540 --> 38:13.660
the connectivity threshold.

38:14.060 --> 38:15.000
When everything

38:15.000 --> 38:15.920
is highly connected,

38:16.340 --> 38:17.420
a global statistic

38:17.420 --> 38:18.840
like forward-reach

38:18.840 --> 38:19.880
is useless.

38:20.200 --> 38:21.580
Because almost every

38:21.580 --> 38:22.180
starting point

38:22.180 --> 38:22.940
can reach a huge

38:22.940 --> 38:23.860
portion of the graph.

38:24.220 --> 38:25.640
Everything touches everything.

38:25.640 --> 38:26.240
Exactly.

38:26.760 --> 38:27.380
The background noise

38:27.380 --> 38:28.120
is deafening.

38:29.120 --> 38:30.100
It beautifully proved

38:30.100 --> 38:30.840
the author's argument.

38:31.540 --> 38:33.100
Global macroscopic statistics

38:33.100 --> 38:33.800
are useless

38:33.800 --> 38:35.020
for finding specific

38:35.020 --> 38:35.880
hidden paths

38:35.880 --> 38:37.440
once a network is dense.

38:38.080 --> 38:39.040
You must use

38:39.040 --> 38:40.660
path-level statistics.

38:41.200 --> 38:42.180
Which flows perfectly

38:42.180 --> 38:42.920
into the final

38:42.920 --> 38:43.720
synthetic test.

38:44.280 --> 38:44.980
Minimum cut

38:44.980 --> 38:46.080
versus the funnel.

38:46.640 --> 38:47.580
The author planted

38:47.580 --> 38:48.660
three different routes

38:48.660 --> 38:49.580
from an entry point

38:49.580 --> 38:50.560
to a sensitive set.

38:50.900 --> 38:51.660
But they specifically

38:51.660 --> 38:53.040
designed it as a funnel.

38:53.040 --> 38:54.560
All three routes

38:54.560 --> 38:55.720
pass through a single

38:55.720 --> 38:57.180
shared hub vertex

38:57.180 --> 38:58.280
before branching out again.

38:59.080 --> 39:00.020
They wanted to test

39:00.020 --> 39:01.080
the edge disjoint

39:01.080 --> 39:02.500
versus vertex disjoint

39:02.500 --> 39:03.080
algorithms.

39:03.200 --> 39:03.460
Right.

39:03.920 --> 39:04.820
This is the ultimate test

39:04.820 --> 39:05.740
of the choke point theory.

39:06.200 --> 39:07.000
When they ran the

39:07.000 --> 39:07.980
edge disjoint count

39:07.980 --> 39:09.040
on this funnel plant,

39:09.460 --> 39:10.440
the algorithm returned

39:10.440 --> 39:11.280
a value of three.

39:11.660 --> 39:12.900
It saw three independent

39:12.900 --> 39:13.820
edges entering

39:13.820 --> 39:14.700
the sensitive set,

39:14.880 --> 39:15.940
so it assumed

39:15.940 --> 39:16.700
there were three

39:16.700 --> 39:17.840
totally independent paths.

39:18.200 --> 39:19.100
But that's a dangerous

39:19.100 --> 39:19.580
illusion.

39:19.580 --> 39:20.820
If a defender

39:20.820 --> 39:21.560
believes that,

39:21.940 --> 39:22.560
they think they have

39:22.560 --> 39:23.220
to patch three

39:23.220 --> 39:24.200
separate vulnerabilities.

39:24.800 --> 39:25.680
But they all pass

39:25.680 --> 39:26.260
through the hub.

39:26.460 --> 39:26.920
Exactly.

39:27.360 --> 39:27.960
And that's why

39:27.960 --> 39:29.200
the vertex disjoint

39:29.200 --> 39:30.360
count was the hero.

39:31.220 --> 39:31.920
It accurately

39:31.920 --> 39:32.720
returned a value

39:32.720 --> 39:33.240
of one.

39:33.640 --> 39:34.280
It recognized

39:34.280 --> 39:35.080
that mathematically,

39:35.520 --> 39:36.340
because all routes

39:36.340 --> 39:37.420
share that single hub,

39:37.860 --> 39:38.580
there is fundamentally

39:38.580 --> 39:39.980
only one internally

39:39.980 --> 39:40.720
disjoint route.

39:40.840 --> 39:41.380
That's awesome.

39:41.620 --> 39:42.140
Even better,

39:42.440 --> 39:43.180
when the algorithm

39:43.180 --> 39:44.220
generated its list

39:44.220 --> 39:45.240
of essential vertices,

39:45.900 --> 39:46.880
that single hub

39:46.880 --> 39:48.020
was successfully listed

39:48.020 --> 39:49.740
as a critical choke point

39:49.740 --> 39:51.320
in 39 out of the 40

39:51.320 --> 39:51.940
test graphs.

39:52.140 --> 39:53.060
39 out of 40.

39:53.120 --> 39:54.660
That is incredible accuracy.

39:55.240 --> 39:56.560
It proves that the math

39:56.560 --> 39:57.920
can actually automate

39:57.920 --> 39:58.940
the discovery of the

39:58.940 --> 40:00.120
most critical vulnerability

40:00.120 --> 40:02.080
points in a massive system.

40:02.580 --> 40:03.360
But of course,

40:03.480 --> 40:04.120
theory is great,

40:04.400 --> 40:05.920
and synthetic sandboxes

40:05.920 --> 40:06.740
are clean and

40:06.740 --> 40:07.700
mathematically pure.

40:07.880 --> 40:08.220
Very true.

40:08.540 --> 40:09.340
What happens when you

40:09.340 --> 40:10.960
take these pristine

40:10.960 --> 40:12.300
mathematical tools

40:12.300 --> 40:13.540
and run them on

40:13.540 --> 40:14.680
real, messy,

40:15.260 --> 40:15.740
chaotic,

40:16.440 --> 40:17.420
human-written code?

40:17.420 --> 40:18.140
Because humans do

40:18.140 --> 40:18.680
weird things

40:18.680 --> 40:19.180
when they code.

40:19.360 --> 40:20.240
They absolutely do.

40:20.800 --> 40:21.400
This brings us

40:21.400 --> 40:21.960
to the climax

40:21.960 --> 40:22.580
of the paper,

40:23.040 --> 40:24.520
the Python pilot study.

40:25.220 --> 40:25.780
The author took

40:25.780 --> 40:26.800
two highly popular

40:26.800 --> 40:28.260
real-world Python projects

40:28.260 --> 40:29.420
to test the methodology

40:29.420 --> 40:30.180
in the wild.

40:30.360 --> 40:30.640
Okay.

40:30.780 --> 40:31.200
What were they?

40:31.400 --> 40:32.440
The first project

40:32.440 --> 40:33.020
was built around

40:33.020 --> 40:33.700
VirtualEnv,

40:33.980 --> 40:35.220
which is a staple tool

40:35.220 --> 40:36.180
for Python developers

40:36.180 --> 40:37.200
for creating

40:37.200 --> 40:38.340
isolated environments.

40:39.040 --> 40:39.420
They included

40:39.420 --> 40:40.240
its dependencies,

40:41.180 --> 40:41.600
dislib,

40:41.960 --> 40:42.500
file lock,

40:42.780 --> 40:43.820
and platform dirs.

40:44.600 --> 40:45.760
The second project

40:45.760 --> 40:46.400
centered on

40:46.400 --> 40:47.100
GitPython,

40:47.100 --> 40:48.460
with its dependencies

40:48.460 --> 40:50.020
GitDB and

40:50.020 --> 40:50.820
SMMF.

40:51.320 --> 40:51.780
Now, they couldn't

40:51.780 --> 40:52.520
just look for

40:52.520 --> 40:54.300
general connectivity.

40:54.520 --> 40:55.380
They needed a target.

40:55.960 --> 40:57.120
They defined a very

40:57.120 --> 40:57.720
specific,

40:58.000 --> 40:59.080
very dangerous,

40:59.660 --> 41:00.400
sensitive set.

41:00.880 --> 41:01.480
They were looking

41:01.480 --> 41:02.440
for any path

41:02.440 --> 41:03.280
that could reach

41:03.280 --> 41:03.980
functions that

41:03.980 --> 41:04.620
launch external

41:04.620 --> 41:05.140
processes.

41:05.560 --> 41:06.100
We are talking

41:06.100 --> 41:06.700
about things like

41:06.700 --> 41:08.020
the subprocess module

41:08.020 --> 41:09.400
or OS.system

41:09.400 --> 41:09.940
in Python.

41:10.380 --> 41:11.340
And for context,

41:11.560 --> 41:12.120
if an attacker

41:12.120 --> 41:12.880
can reach those

41:12.880 --> 41:14.120
modules and control

41:14.120 --> 41:14.640
the input,

41:14.640 --> 41:15.520
they can

41:15.520 --> 41:16.860
essentially execute

41:16.860 --> 41:17.660
arbitrary shell

41:17.660 --> 41:18.260
commands on your

41:18.260 --> 41:18.620
computer.

41:19.060 --> 41:19.720
So total control.

41:20.060 --> 41:20.360
Basically.

41:20.760 --> 41:21.420
They can bypass

41:21.420 --> 41:22.240
Python entirely

41:22.240 --> 41:23.100
and talk directly

41:23.100 --> 41:23.900
to your operating

41:23.900 --> 41:24.320
system.

41:24.800 --> 41:25.400
It's game over.

41:25.720 --> 41:26.260
It's the highest

41:26.260 --> 41:26.920
stakes target

41:26.920 --> 41:27.380
possible.

41:28.140 --> 41:29.220
Now, to analyze

41:29.220 --> 41:30.200
this, we have to

41:30.200 --> 41:31.140
talk about how the

41:31.140 --> 41:31.980
author actually

41:31.980 --> 41:33.120
extracted the map

41:33.120 --> 41:34.020
from the Python code.

41:34.020 --> 41:35.440
They built an

41:35.440 --> 41:36.220
abstract syntax

41:36.220 --> 41:37.220
tree, or AST,

41:37.880 --> 41:38.520
parsing the code

41:38.520 --> 41:39.080
to find the

41:39.080 --> 41:39.940
function definitions

41:39.940 --> 41:40.600
and the calls.

41:41.460 --> 41:42.840
But Python is a

41:42.840 --> 41:43.900
notoriously dynamic

41:43.900 --> 41:44.820
language, which

41:44.820 --> 41:45.580
makes building an

41:45.580 --> 41:46.820
accurate static map

41:46.820 --> 41:48.000
incredibly difficult.

41:48.320 --> 41:49.640
So the author built

41:49.640 --> 41:50.620
two different versions

41:50.620 --> 41:51.260
of the graph to

41:51.260 --> 41:51.580
compare.

41:52.440 --> 41:53.520
First, they built

41:53.520 --> 41:54.380
the strict graph.

41:54.600 --> 41:55.380
The strict graph

41:55.380 --> 41:56.760
is highly conservative.

41:57.360 --> 41:58.180
It only kept

41:58.180 --> 41:59.500
