41:59.500 --> 42:00.420
resolved calls.

42:01.040 --> 42:02.300
If function A

42:02.300 --> 42:03.500
clearly and directly

42:03.500 --> 42:04.500
calls function B

42:04.500 --> 42:05.720
by its exact,

42:06.060 --> 42:07.380
unique name, in a

42:07.380 --> 42:08.160
way the parser can

42:08.160 --> 42:08.840
guarantee without

42:08.840 --> 42:09.600
actually running the

42:09.600 --> 42:10.960
code, it gets an

42:10.960 --> 42:11.180
edge.

42:11.500 --> 42:12.420
What if it's vague?

42:12.920 --> 42:13.760
If there was any

42:13.760 --> 42:15.220
ambiguity, like

42:15.220 --> 42:17.400
dynamic dispatch, or

42:17.400 --> 42:18.600
calling a method

42:18.600 --> 42:19.560
on a variable whose

42:19.560 --> 42:20.400
type is unknown

42:20.400 --> 42:21.960
until runtime, the

42:21.960 --> 42:23.020
strict graph ignored

42:23.020 --> 42:23.560
it completely.

42:23.860 --> 42:24.620
And what were the

42:24.620 --> 42:25.400
findings on this

42:25.400 --> 42:26.840
highly conservative

42:26.840 --> 42:28.240
strict graph?

42:28.660 --> 42:29.520
Well, structurally,

42:29.740 --> 42:30.480
the shadow graphs

42:30.480 --> 42:31.000
were tiny.

42:31.940 --> 42:32.540
For the virtual

42:32.540 --> 42:34.040
N project, the

42:34.040 --> 42:34.820
